.TH BUFFER_REALLOC 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_realloc \- Reallocate the memory of a buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB void
.IB buffer_realloc (buffer* b, size_t ylen);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_realloc\fR function reallocates the memory of a buffer to a new size. It takes a buffer and a new length as arguments. The function reallocates the memory of the buffer to a new size, setting the buffer's length to the new size.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.
.IB size_t
The new length of the buffer.

The \fBbuffer_realloc\fR function is intended to be used to adjust the size of a buffer's memory allocation, such as when adding or removing data from a buffer.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_realloc\fR function to reallocate the memory of a buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    size_t new_length = 1024;

    buffer_realloc(b, new_length);

    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef